# Elsewhere Books Website

Production website for Elsewhere Books, built with React and Vite.

This repository focuses on three engineering goals:
- Fast local iteration (Vite + component-based structure)
- Reliable event data sync (Luma fetch pipeline + cached fallback)
- Low-friction deployment (Vercel + scheduled GitHub Action sync)

## 1. Tech Stack

- Runtime: Node.js 20+
- Frontend: React (functional components + hooks)
- Build tool: Vite
- Data source: Luma Calendar API
- Hosting: Vercel (main branch)
- Automation: GitHub Actions (scheduled events sync)

## 2. Project Structure

.
|- public/
|  |- events.json                 # generated event cache used by frontend
|  |- images/
|- scripts/
|  |- fetch-events.js             # fetch + normalize Luma events
|- src/
|  |- components/                 # page sections and UI primitives
|  |- content/                    # static content records
|  |- hooks/useLumaEvents.js      # fetches /events.json at runtime
|  |- App.jsx                     # main page composition
|  |- main.jsx                    # app entry
|  |- styles.css
|- .github/workflows/fetch-events.yml
|- package.json
|- README

## 3. Local Development

Prerequisites:
- Node.js 20 or newer
- npm 10 or newer

Install dependencies:

npm install

Start development server:

npm run dev

App URLs (default):
- Local: http://localhost:5173
- LAN: printed by Vite in terminal

## 4. Available Scripts

- npm run fetch-events
	- Pulls future and past events from Luma
	- Writes normalized output to public/events.json
- npm run dev
	- Refreshes events first, then starts Vite dev server
- npm run build
	- Refreshes events first, then builds production bundle
- npm run preview
	- Serves the production build locally

## 5. Event Data Pipeline

Event flow:
1. scripts/fetch-events.js calls the Luma calendar API
2. Raw entries are normalized (id/type/time/description/url)
3. Output is written to public/events.json
4. Frontend hook useLumaEvents fetches /events.json

Failure behavior:
- If remote fetch fails and public/events.json exists, cached data is kept
- If remote fetch fails and no cache exists, an empty payload is generated

This keeps production behavior stable even when external API calls fail.

## 6. Environment and Configuration

Current setup does not require API secrets.

- Calendar ID and timezone are configured in scripts/fetch-events.js
- .env exists for future extension, but no required variables are currently enforced

If secrets are introduced later, they should be stored in:
- Local: .env.local (gitignored)
- CI/Hosting: GitHub Actions secrets / Vercel project env vars

## 7. CI and Scheduled Sync

GitHub workflow: .github/workflows/fetch-events.yml

Behavior:
- Runs on schedule (daily UTC cron)
- Also supports manual run via workflow_dispatch
- Commits updated public/events.json when data changes

Operational note:
- This workflow requires write permission to repository contents

## 8. Deployment

- Platform: Vercel
- Source branch: main
- Build command: npm run build
- Output directory: dist

Recommended Vercel settings:
- Node.js version aligned with local/CI (20+)
- Automatic deploys enabled on main

## 9. Engineering Conventions

Branching:
- main is always deployable
- Feature work should use short-lived branches

Commits:
- Prefer Conventional Commits style:
	- feat: new feature
	- fix: bug fix
	- chore: maintenance or tooling
	- docs: documentation only

Pull requests:
- Keep PR scope focused
- Include impact summary and test evidence
- Require green build before merge

Code style:
- Keep components small and composable
- Prefer explicit, readable naming over clever abstractions
- Avoid coupling UI components directly to remote API responses

## 10. Release Checklist

Before merging to main:
1. npm install completed successfully
2. npm run dev works locally
3. npm run build succeeds
4. Key page sections render correctly
5. Event data loads from public/events.json
6. No unintended file changes in commit

## 11. Troubleshooting

Issue: vite is not recognized
- Cause: dependencies not installed
- Fix: run npm install, then npm run dev

Issue: git push returns 403
- Cause: current GitHub account has no write access
- Fix: ask repo owner to add your account as collaborator or push to your fork

Issue: event list is empty
- Check public/events.json exists
- Run npm run fetch-events manually and inspect terminal output
- If remote API is unavailable, verify cached data fallback

## 12. Contact and Ownership

Primary ownership and roadmap should be tracked in repository Issues and PRs to keep decisions auditable.